\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Restricting a split Schur map to sign sectors}
\date{2026-10-29}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative unital ring with invertible two and
#{H=X\oplus Y}. Let #{P=P_X\oplus P_Y}, where
#{P_X^2=I_X}, #{P_Y^2=I_Y}, and let #{Q:H\to H} commute
with #{P}. Write the [typed split components](fdip-001T) as
##{\begin{aligned}
A&:X\to X,&B&:Y\to X,\\
E&:X\to Y,&C&:Y\to Y,\\
Q(x,y)&=(Ax+By,Ex+Cy).
\end{aligned}}
If #{C} is invertible, the split Schur map #{S=A-BC^{-1}E}
preserves both sign sectors of #{P_X}, and
##{S|_{X_\varepsilon}
 =A_\varepsilon-B_\varepsilon C_\varepsilon^{-1}E_\varepsilon.}
Here #{X_\varepsilon=\ker(P_X-\varepsilon I)},
#{Y_\varepsilon=\ker(P_Y-\varepsilon I)}, and each subscript
means restriction to the indicated source and target sectors.}

\proof{
\p{Evaluate #{QP=PQ} on #{(x,0)} and #{(0,y)} and project to
both summands. This gives
##{\begin{aligned}
AP_X&=P_XA,&BP_Y&=P_XB,\\
EP_X&=P_YE,&CP_Y&=P_YC.
\end{aligned}}
Multiplying the last identity on both sides by the inverse of #{C}
gives #{C^{-1}P_Y=P_YC^{-1}}. Each of these identities proves
that its map takes sign-#{\varepsilon} vectors to sign-#{\varepsilon}
vectors. In particular #{C_\varepsilon} is invertible, with inverse
#{C^{-1}|_{Y_\varepsilon}}, since the two inverse equations restrict
to the sector. Finally
##{\begin{aligned}
SP_X&=AP_X-BC^{-1}EP_X\\
 &=P_XA-BP_YC^{-1}E=P_XS.
\end{aligned}}
Restricting the actual composite #{BC^{-1}E:X\to X} now gives
exactly the asserted formula, with no interchange of differently typed
factors.}
}

\p{This is the sign-sector specialization of
[Schur transport](fdip-003E), using the
[module projectors](fdip-003O). Its block convention agrees with
\citet{Appendix A.5.5, pp. 650-651}{boyd2004convex} after exchanging
the source's first pivot to #{C}. The proof here is over modules;
no pairing, self-adjointness, stationary field or probability law has
been assumed. If the prescribed splitting is not preserved by #{P},
the required #{P_X,P_Y} and these restrictions are not supplied.}
